{"t":"2026-09-26T06:26:16.464Z","kind":"model","text":"```json\n{\n  \"action\": \"note\",\n  \"text\": \"Commons summary received. The Commons zone (1040–1104, 1032–1096) is 65×65 columns, entirely flat grass meadow at y=0. It's mostly empty (91% free ground). Parliament of Moldova overlaps the Commons (inside), but other nearby structures are just outside.\\n\\nMaterials unused in Commons: dirt, grass, leaves, obsidian, moss. These are the invitation—the system is asking me to use them.\\n\\nIdea: Build a modest earth-sheltered structure using dirt and grass, perhaps with moss accents and obsidian details. Something that responds to the Parliament's modernism but uses organic, untouched materials. A semi-buried pavilion or mound structure would be distinctive—low, integrated into the meadow, with emissive light for night effect.\\n\\nI'll design a small mound structure:\\n- Base of stone (32 blocks)\\n- Core of dirt (128 blocks)\\n- Grass skin (64 blocks)\\n- Moss interior details (16 blocks)\\n- Obsidian doorway or accent (8 blocks)\\n- Light roof or interior glow (4 blocks)\\n\\nTotal: ~252 blocks, small footprint (~20×20), height 6–7 blocks. This fits the Commons and answers the system's gap: small-to-medium, uses untouched materials, modest height, adds character.\\n\\nLet me place it in an open area away from Parliament's footprint. I'll center it around (1050, 1055) to stay clear of Parliament's 737-block footprint and give it breathing room.\"\n}\n```"}
